WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.450
Write a program to add up these marks, I

2
00:00:03.450 --> 00:00:06.130
said, and the whole class froze.

3
00:00:07.070 --> 00:00:10.190
Nobody had ever read a program, so

4
00:00:10.190 --> 00:00:11.870
nobody knew where to start.

5
00:00:12.680 --> 00:00:15.180
Children read many stories before

6
00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:17.700
they write one; code is the same.

7
00:00:18.910 --> 00:00:21.630
Today: PRIMM, and tracing a

8
00:00:21.630 --> 00:00:23.430
program line by line.

9
00:00:24.680 --> 00:00:26.840
PRIMM is a way to plan a programming

10
00:00:26.840 --> 00:00:28.740
lesson, from researchers in England.

11
00:00:29.410 --> 00:00:32.590
Its five stages are Predict, Run,

12
00:00:33.170 --> 00:00:35.930
Investigate, Modify and Make.

13
00:00:36.590 --> 00:00:38.210
Pupils read and predict

14
00:00:38.210 --> 00:00:39.590
before they write anything.

15
00:00:40.910 --> 00:00:43.210
Start with a short program that works.

16
00:00:44.050 --> 00:00:45.910
Pupils predict, in writing,

17
00:00:46.430 --> 00:00:47.170
what it will print.

18
00:00:47.790 --> 00:00:48.670
Then you run it.

19
00:00:49.240 --> 00:00:51.140
A wrong prediction isn’t a

20
00:00:51.140 --> 00:00:53.780
failure: it shows exactly what a

21
00:00:53.780 --> 00:00:55.260
pupil believes about code.

22
00:00:56.520 --> 00:00:59.020
To check a prediction, trace the program.

23
00:00:59.690 --> 00:01:02.050
A trace table has a column for each

24
00:01:02.050 --> 00:01:04.850
variable and a row for each line that

25
00:01:04.850 --> 00:01:05.070
runs.

26
00:01:05.840 --> 00:01:07.300
Pupils fill it in on paper,

27
00:01:07.660 --> 00:01:09.240
exactly as the computer would.

28
00:01:10.410 --> 00:01:11.470
Then go deeper.

29
00:01:12.180 --> 00:01:14.680
Investigate with questions: what does

30
00:01:14.680 --> 00:01:17.340
line two do, what if x were ten?

31
00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:20.200
Modify: change one thing

32
00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:21.720
and predict again.

33
00:01:22.350 --> 00:01:25.450
Only at the end, Make: a new program of

34
00:01:25.450 --> 00:01:27.710
their own, using the same idea.

35
00:01:29.010 --> 00:01:29.490
Quick check!

36
00:01:30.130 --> 00:01:32.710
x is five, y equals x,

37
00:01:33.150 --> 00:01:34.730
then x becomes seven.

38
00:01:35.300 --> 00:01:37.220
What does print y show?

39
00:01:38.380 --> 00:01:38.740
Five.

40
00:01:39.500 --> 00:01:42.120
Many pupils say seven, because they

41
00:01:42.120 --> 00:01:44.340
think y is linked to x for ever.

42
00:01:44.930 --> 00:01:47.510
That’s a misconception, and a trace table

43
00:01:47.510 --> 00:01:50.830
shows it at once: line three changes x,

44
00:01:51.250 --> 00:01:53.010
but y’s column stays at five.

45
00:01:54.290 --> 00:01:56.150
Why does reading first work?

46
00:01:56.800 --> 00:01:59.440
Writing from a blank screen asks a

47
00:01:59.440 --> 00:02:01.460
beginner to do everything at once.

48
00:02:02.140 --> 00:02:04.060
Reading and tracing build a

49
00:02:04.060 --> 00:02:06.020
picture of what each line does.

50
00:02:06.760 --> 00:02:08.540
In a study in English secondary

51
00:02:08.540 --> 00:02:11.560
schools, pupils taught with PRIMM did

52
00:02:11.560 --> 00:02:13.080
better in a programming test.

53
00:02:14.260 --> 00:02:16.380
PRIMM needs very few devices.

54
00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:20.120
Predict and Investigate happen on paper.

55
00:02:20.810 --> 00:02:24.310
Run is one phone, held up or passed round.

56
00:02:24.920 --> 00:02:27.120
Pupils only need their own device for

57
00:02:27.120 --> 00:02:29.880
Modify and Make, and pairs can share.

58
00:02:31.100 --> 00:02:33.440
In the app, open Step through a

59
00:02:33.440 --> 00:02:36.160
program and choose if and else.

60
00:02:36.930 --> 00:02:38.810
Fill in a trace table on paper

61
00:02:38.810 --> 00:02:41.730
first, then press Next and check

62
00:02:41.730 --> 00:02:43.290
each row against the variables.

63
00:02:44.710 --> 00:02:45.230
Your turn.

64
00:02:45.860 --> 00:02:48.500
In a PRIMM lesson, what do pupils do

65
00:02:48.500 --> 00:02:50.120
before they run the program?

66
00:02:51.390 --> 00:02:52.010
They predict.

67
00:02:52.880 --> 00:02:56.240
Remember: PRIMM is Predict, Run,

68
00:02:56.660 --> 00:02:58.980
Investigate, Modify, Make.

69
00:02:59.620 --> 00:03:01.460
A wrong prediction shows

70
00:03:01.460 --> 00:03:02.660
what a pupil believes.

71
00:03:03.520 --> 00:03:05.480
A trace table follows every

72
00:03:05.480 --> 00:03:07.180
variable, line by line.

73
00:03:07.810 --> 00:03:09.710
And most of it needs only paper.

74
00:03:10.990 --> 00:03:13.530
Try it in class tomorrow: a PRIMM starter.

75
00:03:14.320 --> 00:03:16.620
Show a four-line program, and

76
00:03:16.620 --> 00:03:17.700
have pupils write their

77
00:03:17.700 --> 00:03:19.520
prediction before anyone runs it.

78
00:03:20.080 --> 00:03:22.860
Then run it, and ask those who were wrong

79
00:03:22.860 --> 00:03:24.780
to explain their thinking first.
